=========================================================
CREATIONAL DESIGN PATTERNS (INTERVIEW QUICK REVISION)
=========================================================

---------------------------------------------------------
1. SINGLETON
---------------------------------------------------------

Purpose:
- Ensure only ONE object exists.
- Provide global access to it.

Need:
- Shared resource should have only one instance.  *****************

Core Idea:
One Object + Global Access

Real-world Examples:
- Logger
- Configuration Manager
- Cache Manager
- Thread Pool Manager
- Database Connection Manager

Interview Example:
Application-wide Logger

Pros:
✔ One instance only
✔ Saves memory
✔ Global access

Cons:
✘ Global state
✘ Harder to unit test
✘ Can become a bottleneck

Best Modern Implementation:
Meyers' Singleton (static local variable)

Interview One-Liner:
"Singleton ensures only one instance of a class exists and provides a global access point."


---------------------------------------------------------
2. FACTORY METHOD
---------------------------------------------------------

Purpose:
- Hide object creation.
- Let subclasses decide which object to create.

Need:
- Client should not create concrete objects directly. *************

Core Idea:
Client → Factory → Product

Real-world Examples:
- Payment Gateway (UPI, Card, PayPal)
- Database Drivers
- Logger Creation
- Notification Service
- GUI Widgets

Interview Example:
Vehicle Factory
(CarFactory, BikeFactory)

Pros:
✔ Loose coupling
✔ Follows Open/Closed Principle
✔ Easy to extend

Cons:
✘ More classes
✘ Slightly more complex

Interview One-Liner:
"Factory Method delegates object creation to factory subclasses instead of letting the client create objects directly."


---------------------------------------------------------
3. ABSTRACT FACTORY
---------------------------------------------------------

Purpose:
- Create families of related objects.

Need:
- Multiple related objects must always be created together.

Core Idea:
Factory creates a COMPLETE FAMILY.

Real-world Examples:
- Cross-platform GUI
  Windows:
    - WindowsButton
    - WindowsScrollbar

  Mac:
    - MacButton
    - MacScrollbar

- Mobile UI Themes
- Game Engine (Desert Theme, Snow Theme)
- Database Families
- Cloud Provider SDKs (AWS, Azure, GCP)

Interview Example:
GUI Framework

WindowsFactory
    -> WindowsButton
    -> WindowsScrollbar

MacFactory
    -> MacButton
    -> MacScrollbar

Pros:
✔ Keeps related objects together
✔ Prevents mixing incompatible objects
✔ Loose coupling
✔ Easy to switch entire product family

Cons:
✘ More interfaces/classes
✘ Harder to add new product types

Interview One-Liner:
"Abstract Factory creates families of related objects without exposing their concrete classes."


---------------------------------------------------------
4. BUILDER
---------------------------------------------------------

Purpose:
- Build complex objects step-by-step.

Need:
- Object has many fields or optional parameters.

Core Idea:
Construction separated from representation.

Flow:
Client
   ↓
Director
   ↓
Builder
   ↓
Product

Real-world Examples:
- Car Builder
- Pizza Builder
- House Builder
- SQL Query Builder
- HTTP Request Builder
- Email Builder
- Report/PDF Builder

Interview Example:
Car Builder

SportsCarBuilder

LuxuryCarBuilder

Director builds the car.

Pros:
✔ Step-by-step construction
✔ Avoids huge constructors
✔ Cleaner code
✔ Same process builds different products

Cons:
✘ More classes
✘ Slightly more boilerplate

Interview One-Liner:
"Builder constructs complex objects step-by-step while separating construction from representation."


=========================================================
WHEN TO USE WHICH?
=========================================================

Singleton
→ One shared object

Example:
Logger


Factory Method
→ Need ONE object, but type varies

Example:
Vehicle Factory


Abstract Factory
→ Need a FAMILY of related objects

Example:
Windows Button + Windows Scrollbar


Builder
→ Need to construct a COMPLEX object step-by-step

Example:
Build a Car with engine, body, seats


